Remediation of CVE-2013-3900. This attack is still active and relevant as per Microsofts last update in December 2024 "Microsoft does not plan to enforce the stricter verification behavior as a default functionality on supported releases of Microsoft Windows."
Description of Vulnerability: An anonymous attacker could exploit the vulnerability by modifying an existing signed executable file to leverage unverified portions of the file in such a way as to add malicious code to the file without invalidating the signature. An attacker who successfully exploited this vulnerability could take complete control of an affected system. An attacker could then install programs; view, change, or delete data; or create new accounts with full user rights.


This Powershell Remediation has been tested as of Q1 2026 and works as of time tested.
Powershell script as follows: 

#Requires -RunAsAdministrator
<#
.SYNOPSIS
    Enables Certificate Padding Check (EnableCertPaddingCheck) on Windows devices.

.DESCRIPTION
    Remediates CVE-2013-3900 — WinVerifyTrust Signature Validation Vulnerability.
    This vulnerability allows attackers to embed malicious code into the padding
    space of a legitimately signed PE file (.exe, .dll, .sys) without invalidating
    the Authenticode digital signature. Enabling this check forces Windows to reject
    such non-conforming binaries as unsigned.

    What this script does:
      - Sets EnableCertPaddingCheck = 1 (REG_DWORD) in both required registry paths:
          64-bit : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Cryptography\Wintrust\Config
          WOW64  : HKLM\SOFTWARE\Wow6432Node\Microsoft\Cryptography\Wintrust\Config
      - Creates the registry key path if it does not exist
      - Skips keys already correctly configured
      - Verifies both values after writing
      - No reboot required — change takes effect immediately

    Value type: REG_DWORD per Microsoft's latest guidance (updated November 2024).
    Note: REG_SZ "1" is also accepted by Windows and does NOT need to be changed
    if already present — per Microsoft's official statement.

    References:
      CVE        : https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2013-3900
      Advisory   : https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security-updates/securityadvisories/2014/2915720
      CIS Control: CIS Windows Benchmark — MS Security Guide ADMX

    Exit codes (NinjaOne policy evaluation):
      0 = Already compliant, or successfully remediated
      1 = Failed to set one or both registry values

.NOTES
    CVE         : CVE-2013-3900
    CVSS Score  : 7.4 (High)
    No Reboot   : True
    Run As      : System
    PowerShell  : 5.1+
    Safe to re-run : Yes (idempotent)
#>

# ── Registry Paths ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
$RegKeys = @(
    @{
        Path        = "HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Cryptography\Wintrust\Config"
        Description = "64-bit (native)"
    },
    @{
        Path        = "HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Wow6432Node\Microsoft\Cryptography\Wintrust\Config"
        Description = "32-bit WOW64 compatibility"
    }
)

$ValueName = "EnableCertPaddingCheck"
$ValueData = 1
$ValueType = "DWORD"   # REG_DWORD — Microsoft's preferred type as of Nov 2024

# ── Helper: Logging ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
function Write-Log {
    param([string]$Message, [string]$Level = "INFO")
    $ts = Get-Date -Format "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"
    Write-Output "[$ts] [$Level] $Message"
}

# ── Helper: Check if a value is already correctly configured ───────────────────
function Test-RegValue {
    param([string]$Path, [string]$Name, [int]$ExpectedData)
    try {
        $current = Get-ItemProperty -Path $Path -Name $Name -ErrorAction Stop
        $raw = $current.$Name
        # Accept both DWORD 1 and REG_SZ "1" as compliant per Microsoft guidance
        return ($raw -eq $ExpectedData -or $raw -eq "$ExpectedData")
    } catch {
        return $false
    }
}

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# MAIN
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
Write-Log "================================================"
Write-Log "CVE-2013-3900 — Certificate Padding Check"
Write-Log "EnableCertPaddingCheck Remediation Script"
Write-Log "================================================"
Write-Log "Setting: $ValueName = $ValueData ($ValueType)"
Write-Log "Scope  : 64-bit + WOW64 registry hives"
Write-Log "------------------------------------------------"

$allSucceeded = $true
$changesNeeded = 0
$changesApplied = 0

foreach ($key in $RegKeys) {
    $path = $key.Path
    $desc = $key.Description

    Write-Log "Processing [$desc]"
    Write-Log "  Path: $path"

    # ── Check current state ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    $alreadyCompliant = Test-RegValue -Path $path -Name $ValueName -ExpectedData $ValueData

    if ($alreadyCompliant) {
        Write-Log "  [COMPLIANT] $ValueName is already correctly set. No change needed." "INFO"
        continue
    }

    $changesNeeded++
    Write-Log "  [ACTION] Value missing or incorrect. Applying remediation..." "WARN"

    # ── Ensure registry key path exists ──────────────────────────────────────
    if (-not (Test-Path $path)) {
        Write-Log "  Registry key path does not exist. Creating..."
        try {
            New-Item -Path $path -Force -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
            Write-Log "  Registry key created."
        } catch {
            Write-Log "  FAILED to create registry key: $_" "ERROR"
            $allSucceeded = $false
            continue
        }
    }

    # ── Set the value ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    try {
        # Use -Force to overwrite if a REG_SZ "1" was previously set by older guidance
        New-ItemProperty -Path $path `
                         -Name $ValueName `
                         -Value $ValueData `
                         -PropertyType $ValueType `
                         -Force `
                         -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
        Write-Log "  Value written successfully."
    } catch {
        Write-Log "  FAILED to write registry value: $_" "ERROR"
        $allSucceeded = $false
        continue
    }

    # ── Verify the write ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    $verified = Test-RegValue -Path $path -Name $ValueName -ExpectedData $ValueData
    if ($verified) {
        Write-Log "  [VERIFIED] $ValueName = $ValueData confirmed in registry."
        $changesApplied++
    } else {
        Write-Log "  [FAILED] Value written but verification failed. Manual inspection required." "ERROR"
        $allSucceeded = $false
    }

    Write-Log "  --------"
}

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# RESULT SUMMARY
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
Write-Log "================================================"

if ($allSucceeded) {
    if ($changesNeeded -eq 0) {
        Write-Log "[COMPLIANT] Both registry values were already correctly configured."
        Write-Log "No changes required. Device is protected against CVE-2013-3900."
    } else {
        Write-Log "[SUCCESS] Remediation applied: $changesApplied of $changesNeeded value(s) set."
        Write-Log "[COMPLIANT] Certificate Padding Check is now active on this device."
        Write-Log "Windows will now reject Authenticode-signed binaries with extraneous"
        Write-Log "padding data — protecting against CVE-2013-3900 exploit attempts."
        Write-Log "NOTE: No reboot required. Change is effective immediately."
    }
    Write-Log "================================================"
    exit 0
} else {
    Write-Log "[FAILED] One or more registry values could not be set." "ERROR"
    Write-Log "This device may still be vulnerable to CVE-2013-3900." "ERROR"
    Write-Log "Review the output above and check registry permissions." "ERROR"
    Write-Log "================================================"
    exit 1
}
